WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.120
Hubel and Wiesel recorded from single cells in the visual cortex of anaesthetized

2
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:11.220
cats. They projected spots of light onto a screen.

3
00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.320
Spots drive the retina well.

4
00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.260
In the cortex, spots often did little.

5
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.620
Bars at the right orientation drove cells hard.

6
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.120
Why would a bar win?

7
00:00:21.933 --> 00:00:26.653
A receptive field is the region of the visual field where a stimulus changes a

8
00:00:26.653 --> 00:00:31.193
neuron's firing. Hubel and Wiesel recorded with an electrode outside the cell.

9
00:00:31.853 --> 00:00:34.693
They mapped each field with spots, slits and bars.

10
00:00:35.553 --> 00:00:38.773
Moving a stimulus and counting spikes shows where the field lies.

11
00:00:39.867 --> 00:00:42.487
The first class they described was the simple cell.

12
00:00:43.107 --> 00:00:47.187
A simple cell has separate ON and OFF subregions, side by side.

13
00:00:47.887 --> 00:00:50.067
Light in an ON region excites the cell.

14
00:00:50.827 --> 00:00:54.687
Light in an OFF region inhibits it, and removing that light excites it.

15
00:00:55.247 --> 00:00:58.767
Light that covered an ON and an OFF region together tended to cancel.

16
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.900
The subregions are elongated, so the orientation of a bar is critical.

17
00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.080
A bar aligned with the ON strip drove the cell.

18
00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:09.440
A bar across the strip barely did.

19
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.920
Turn the bar, and the response changes.

20
00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:15.653
Here is a made-up recording from a V1 cell.

21
00:01:16.153 --> 00:01:19.113
The bar sits at the best position, and many trials are averaged.

22
00:01:19.753 --> 00:01:22.693
With no bar, the cell fires at 2 spikes/s.

23
00:01:23.293 --> 00:01:25.393
Six orientations draw a tuning curve.

24
00:01:25.813 --> 00:01:29.713
The best orientation is 90 degrees, at 40 spikes/s.

25
00:01:30.153 --> 00:01:34.773
At half height the curve is about 67 degrees wide, an interpolated estimate.

26
00:01:35.393 --> 00:01:37.893
The orientation index is 0.90.

27
00:01:38.633 --> 00:01:43.793
Near 1 means a strong preference for the best orientation over the orthogonal one.

28
00:01:44.433 --> 00:01:46.633
It does not by itself give the tuning width.

29
00:01:47.667 --> 00:01:51.367
Complex cells keep the orientation preference but tolerate position.

30
00:01:51.947 --> 00:01:54.807
A correctly oriented bar drives them over much of the field.

31
00:01:55.327 --> 00:01:57.707
No separate ON and OFF regions can be mapped.

32
00:01:58.407 --> 00:02:02.287
Hubel and Wiesel suggested that geniculate cells in a row converge on a simple cell,

33
00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:04.847
and simple cells converge on a complex cell.

34
00:02:05.427 --> 00:02:09.147
A proposal then; the first step later held up, the second is debated.

35
00:02:09.927 --> 00:02:15.207
Keep the rule: in cat visual cortex, bars at the right angle drive cells that spots

36
00:02:15.207 --> 00:02:15.907
often miss.
